  "findings": [
    "Kernel-paced departure (SO_TXTIME + fq, 30 ms look-ahead) removes the scheduling leak: in isolation the per-window count is exactly constant (AUC 0.500, p = 1, every run) where a timed loop leaks.",
    "It exposes a power-state side channel: with the CPU idle, the release timer wakes late from deep C-states, so packets are LESS regular when idle (gap AUC 0.997-1.000). Cores kept awake by busy loops remove it (no leak in 3 of 3, p 0.08-0.79); a timed loop still leaks completely with awake cores.",
    "Power QoS is not a substitute for awake cores: 20 us leaks in 3 of 3, 0 us in 2 of 3 (gap AUC 0.63-0.92). Busy loops also raise clock frequency; the residue may be frequency, not idle depth (untested).",
    "On the real carrier, the cloak's wire clock turns perfect separation (today: p = 0.0005 every run, count AUC 0.92-1.00 or gap AUC 0.001-0.07) into marginal: no leak by the corrected test in 3 of 3 runs without power QoS and 2 of 3 with it, but count p reaches 0.005 in two runs and largest-gap p 0.0005 in one.",
    "The residue sits at load transitions: windows that continue a state carry the clock's exact count (3,264), windows right after the load stops come up short and right after it starts run over. Next: cloak counters for skipped slots and late sends, to tell stalls longer than the look-ahead from slot/packet desynchronisation."
  ],
  "status": "Not a fix yet: the wire clock stays off by default, and fast is still not claimed against website fingerprinting on a busy CPU."
}
